# Leave these alone, they are not language:
#
#   * the level prefixes — Error:, Warning:, Notice:, Info:, Crit: — which
#     say how bad it is, not what it says, and which callers read;
#   * anything of the shape name=value or name[0]='value', which is output
#     meant for a program;
#   * option names (--keyservers), action names (certify, print_secret) and
#     property names (FN:, UID:urn:eid:), which are typed, not read;
#   * fingerprints, and the words u4 and u5.
#
# Keep the columns of the OPTIONS blocks lined up: they are read as a table,
# and a line that folds takes the alignment with it. Do not make a line wider
# than the English one it replaces — po/check-width.py says when you have.
#
# Say what an irreversible thing costs. Where the English says a revocation
# cannot be undone, or that a wrong code spends one of three attempts, that
# sentence is the only place anybody learns it — a shorter translation that
# drops the consequence is a worse translation.
#
